        "prefix_token_count": 834,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Steve gives the value of \\(a\\) as 11, and Jon says there are two possible values of \\(c\\). This means there must be another combination of roots that also satisfies the conditions. Testing other combinations, we find:\n\\[ p = 1, q = 4, r = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad p = 4, q = 1, r = 6. \\]\nIn this case, \\(pqr = 1 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 = 24\\), so \\(c = 2 \\cdot 24 = 48\\)."
      },
      {
